This release of pd-ksh (edition #11) for OS9/68k is based onpd-ksh v4.3 as distributed on Usenet.This file covers only topics which are specific to this port.More information about ksh may be found in ksh.1 (roff-format),which is included here, and ksh.man (a poorly formatted versionof ksh.1..).  Again, these cover only the details specific topd-ksh (as opposed to David Korns ksh).  If you want to know more,look for AT&T's manual or look for the book by Bolsky/Korn, The Kornshell, Prentice Hall, ISBN 0-13-516972-0. Most features ofksh88 as described there also apply to pd-ksh.1. Options========== The following options have been added.  They can be set as command line arguments upon invocation as well as with the 'set' builtin command:    -E    -E <file>       add _errmsg()-style error reporting.  If                    <file> is omitted, /dd/sys/errmsg and                    /h0/sys/errmsg (in this order) are                    checked and used, if present.                     As with all options, case is significant.    -l              reqire 'logout' to exit the shell. The profiles                    (see below) are read before starting and the                    shell script '~/.logout.ksh' in the user's                    home directory, if present, is executed                    on exit.    -t              track the shell's process of locating a                    command by reporting what is searched for and                    where it is found.    -z              start child shells under srcdbg control.                     This is a debugging feature; it will be removed.    -w, -y          YADF (yet another debugging flag).  Their meanings                    will change according to which aspect is currently                    under test.  You should avoid to set them.2. Profiles=========== Then following profiles are read when ksh is started. If they are not accessible, no error message is given.    If the shell is started with the 'l' command line option (i. e.,    as login shell),  the following  scripts (if present) are executed before     entering interactive mode:        /dd/sys/profile.ksh     replaces  UNIX /etc/profile        ~/.profile.ksh          replaces  UNIX .profile    They are read in by the shell in this order.       ~/.logout.ksh is executed before exiting.    In any case, $ENV (if present and pointing to a valid path) is expanded    and executed on all invocations even if the shell is non-interactive.    Finally, if ksh is interactive (stdin, -out and -err are connected    to a scf type device) and expects its commands to come from stdin,     $ENVI is expanded and executed (if present and pointing to a valid     script file). Use    * /dd/SYS/profile.ksh         to set and export global variables which        are commen to all users on this system (PATH, printer        and network definitions etc).    * ~/.profile.ksh        to set and export global variables which are specific        to the user (e.g. $TERM)    * $ENV        to define functions, aliases, and default options that        you want to apply to all ksh invocations    * $ENVI        for functions, aliases, edit key bindings and options you need        for interactive use. Sample profiles are found in ../SAMPLES.3. OS9/68k - specific features============================== 3.1  Added built-in commands ----------------------------  chx:   Change excution directory.  The syntax is the same as with          the 'cd' command.  The current and previous execution directory are         reflected by $ODIR and $OLDODIR.  Both are read-only.  setpr:  with one argument, the shell's own priority is changed.          If two or more arguments are given, the priorities of the          process numbers in the following arguments are changed to          the value of the last argument.  logout: Same as 'exit', but executes ~/.logout.ksh before          exiting.  If the -l flag is set, 'logout' is required          to exit; 'exit' prints an error message.  isize:  override default file segment allocation size. This           command may be used to avoid file segmentation on large           RBF and PIPE type output files.          Without option ('isize <kBytes>'), it affects the next path           which is newly created by ksh; in the form 'isize -a <kBytes>'           all subsequent newly created paths are affected. Use 'isize 0'          to return to the default allocation size as predefined          by your RBF or PIPE path descriptor.                    Example:                isize 3000      # pre-extend to 3 MB                exec 4>x	# create() x with initial size = 1MB                compress -dc foo.Z 1>&4   # decompress to file x                exec 4>&-	# close x          If you use this command, ksh uses create() instead of          creat() to create the next or all subsequent files.          Note that if you redirect an existing descriptor to          a file or pipe, dup() will be used instead, so          isize will neither be used nor reset.          In other words: isize can only be used on file descriptors          3..9 and only if they are closed.  3.2 Additions to the command line syntax  ----------------------------------------   '^' has been added as a special character.  It must be immediately   followed by either 'p' or 'm', to set the sub-process's priority and   additional memory, respectively.   Both combinations must be followed by a numeric argument, which    may be optionally seperated by blanks.  Examples:      procs ^p100          run 'procs' with a priority of 100      procs ^m33           run procs with 33k of additional memory   The same result is achieved by writing:     procs ^p 100     procs ^m 33   Note that because '^' has been redefined to be a special character,   you must quote it in any other context (e.g. emacs key-bindings).   Tilde expansion: a '~' which follows a '=' is always expanded   (This is the 'copy' hack: 'copy -w=~/any/directory' would not   be expanded without it).   The built-in print (echo) command also interprets hex escape   sequences.  They consist of the character "\x", which   must be followed by 1 or 2 hex digits.  The first non-hex digit    terminates the sequence.  The sequence        print "\x31u"    evaluates to '1' followed by 'u';        print "\x7v"    to a Control-G followed by the character 'v'.  But       print "\xy"    however means a quoted 'x' (whichs stands for itself, because   it is not followed by a hex number) followed by 'y', resulting    in the printed string "xy". Finally,       print "\x7v"   and       print \\x7v   are the same due to the shell's command line interpretation rules.  3.3 Environment  ---------------  If $_sh does not exist, it is created with a value of zero and  type integer and export.  If it already exists, its value is   incremented by 1 at each invocation. I use it in my  prompt (PS1='[$PWD] ${_sh#0}> ').  $ODIR and $OLDODIR reflect the current and previous  execution directory.  Initially, both are the same.  If $ENVI exists and points to a valid file, it is used as  path to a script file which is executed any time ksh is  invoced for intercative use. This was done mainly for speed  reasons; you might as well use $ENV and check $- for both 's'  and 'i' flags being set.  $SHELL is set to the shell's name ("ksh").  $PORT, if not present, is set to "/term". This is for debugging  startup shells; since $PORT is exists in all other cases,  this should pose no restrictions.    3.4 Command searching and execution  -----------------------------------  The mechanism of locating a command differs from what you  may be used to.  The changes were mainly due to the fact that the  user should not need to know if a command is a shell script  or if it is written in another programming language.  All commands (loadable files and ksh scripts) are always searched the  same way: along the $PATH variable. The execution directory is only  searched for the command if it is part of $PATH or if $PATH is missing.  If a file with execute permission attribute is found but without   appropriate read permission, it is assumed to be a module and   executed (scripts don't make much sense if the shell can't read them).    If it has both execute and read permissions, it will be loaded and   executed only if its first word is 0x4afc (start of module header),  else it is considered a shell script and executed as such.   Files without execute permisson are ignored during command search.  All executable files must have the execute permission bit set.  This  includes scripts, which must also have the appropriate read  attribute.  The shell itself looks for what type they are   and executes them accordingly (instead of telling you "can't   execute 'J'"...)  The PATH variable is expanded before being used (this differs from  UNIX pd-ksh).  This means that if your PATH is defined as    PATH=.:$ODIR:/h0/CMDS/h0/USR/CMDS:/h0/ETC/CMDS  your search path will not follow if you change your execution directory  using the chx command, but instead will stay at what it was defined when you  started.  However if you define PATH as follows (note the '\' before  $ODIR):    PATH=.:\$ODIR:/h0/CMDS/h0/USR/CMDS:/h0/ETC/CMDS  your current execution directory is always searched for even if it  has changed.  The same is true for:    PATH='.:$ODIR:/h0/CMDS/h0/USR/CMDS:/h0/ETC/CMDS'  If no $PATH variable exists, the current exec dir is used  instead (for use as startup shell).3.4 Terminal modes  There are two different sets of terminal modes: the one under  which ksh executes, and another one for user programs.  Initially, both are the same.   They are copied from what  is current when ksh is started up.  Anytime a command exits, (most of) its modes are saved and ksh's   modes are restored.  Next time you execute a command, the previous  command's tmodes are restored.  This is done so that you can  change (via tmode) a command's terminal modes without affecting  the shell - and to prevent a command from leaving the  parent with unusable modes (ever had 'com' aborting without  restoring eol?).  ksh always keeps its modes.  Currently, there is no way to change   ksh's tmodes - even the baudrate will stay at what it was   at invocation.  This doesn't make much sense and will be changed.  5. Some final notes (bugs and athers..)=======================================If a process which is part of a command pipeline aborts, allprocesses before it are killed instead of letting themhang around. Signals received by subshells will be passed to the parent.The same is true if a subprocess' exit status indicates that is wasaborted by signals #2 and #3 (quit and interrupt). This is doneto emulate the unix signal mechanism, thereby allowing theparent shell to take appropriate action. This introducessame nasties with programs using exit values #2 and #3. It allows,however, scripts to be aborted, as in:    for i in *.r; do del *; done(don't try this before you've seen the typo!!). Without thismechanism, your directory would have been gone, since chancesare poor that the abort abort signal you type is receiced by kshand not by del.the test command works as described, except for the following:    test -c is true on SCF and PTTY type devices    test -b is true on SBF devices    test -p is true on PIPE devices    test -S is true on SOCKET devicesand its bugs are as follows:    test -r, -w, and -x don't work if the arguemnt is a directory (result            is always 0)    test -t always returns truethe following tests make no sense:    test -u,g,k always return true if the device exists    test -L  always retuns true.The only editing mode that has been tested is emcas mode(set -o emacs).  raw and vi mode have not been checked yet.The default editor for the fc command is 'me'.The values retuned  by 'time' and 'times' are now correct. Thereis no time information on child processes available in OSK for theparent, so they show up as 0.Note that with emacs key bindings, you must escape the '^'.The shell accepts 8-bit characters in emacs mode.The trap commands cannot work in OS9 as they do in *IX. Becareful.The current OSK edition is 11 (use 'ident -q <path>' to see it).5. Compiling============I've used the standard microware K&R - Compiler, butyou need dmake to compile the current version.  Copy ../SYS/startup.mkinto your /dd/SYS directory before starting dmake.  You should nothave a closer look at it - it's still a hack and will be cleanedup some time in the future (Real Soon Now..).  The dmake andksh binaries are found in the ../CMDS directory; dmakedocumentation is included here.If you get many compile time messages about repeated inclusion of header files, edit the offending .h's the usual way (#ifndef <..>.h #define <..>.h <original contents> #endif). You might as well choose to live with those warnings ortry another preprocessor.Chances are good that it will not compile on your system due to some missing #include or library.  Even if you can fix it, please report it to my mail address so it can be fixed.The executables (Directory CMDS) are compiled with OSK V 2.4. For olderversions, you will have to recompile them if you get a 'cio mismatch'error, or you might try the following patch:    1.  look for the string '**** cio traphandler mismatch ****' in the        code.    2.  $62 bytes before, you will find the following sequence:        $7609 $2683 $262e    3.  The lower byte in $7609 (the 09) must be replaced by a         number which is lower or equal to your cio's revision        (08 for OSK 2.3).    4.  don't forget to update the module CRC after your patch        has been done.I never tried this patch with pd-ksh, but it worked well with other programs some months ago when I was still using 2.3.Comments and bug reports are welcome to the following address:~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Heike C. Zimmerer                                       hcz@reto.stgt.sub.orgreto Industrieelektronik GmbH      Kornbergstr. 18           7000 Stuttgart 1